WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
RLHF.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:04.834
Reinforcement learning from human feedback.

3
00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:07.367
You should be able to draw it on a whiteboard.

4
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:09.900
Here's the big picture.

5
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.700
First, humans rank pairs of model responses: this one is more helpful.

6
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.600
Next, a reward model is trained to predict those preferences.

7
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:24.500
Then the language model is optimized against that reward, using an algorithm called PPO.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.367
Let's trace one prompt through it.

9
00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.767
The policy writes several responses to the same prompt.

10
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:32.933
Humans rank them, best to worst.

11
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:37.755
The reward model learns from those rankings and scores each response.

12
00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:40.967
PPO then updates the policy toward higher scores.

13
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:45.025
A KL penalty acts like a leash to a frozen reference copy,

14
00:00:45.025 --> 00:00:48.200
or the policy drifts into high scoring gibberish.

15
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.133
That means four models in memory.

16
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:54.533
The policy is the one being trained, and it generates.

17
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:56.833
The reward model scores the replies.

18
00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:59.567
The value head estimates future reward.

19
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:02.333
And the frozen reference anchors the policy.

20
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:04.733
Here they are in code.

21
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:07.167
The four roles are named one per line.

22
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.703
The trainer takes all four by keyword,

23
00:01:09.703 --> 00:01:15.000
plus a small coefficient that controls how strongly drift from the reference is penalized.

24
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.400
Why does this matter when you pick a model?

25
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:24.900
Its model card says aligned with RLHF, or DPO on a feedback dataset.

26
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.500
That tells you it was optimized for ranked human preference.

27
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.067
Your flight ops users will notice it: concise, hedged, helpful sounding summaries.

28
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:36.026
Let's recap.

29
00:01:36.026 --> 00:01:39.633
Humans rank replies, and a reward model learns from them.

30
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.400
PPO optimizes the policy against that reward.

31
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.833
A KL penalty keeps the policy near its reference.

32
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:50.315
Four models make it expensive and finicky.

33
00:01:50.315 --> 00:01:51.300
Next up: DPO.
